[00033.869072] 
[00033.869072]     -dont_use <cell_name>
[00033.869073]         avoid usage of the technology cell <cell_name> when mapping the design.
[00033.869073]         this option can be used multiple times with different cell names and
[00033.869074]         supports simple glob patterns in the cell name.
[00033.869075]         only supported with Liberty cell libraries.
[00033.869075] 
[00033.869075]     -genlib <file>
[00033.869076]         generate netlists for the specified cell library (using the SIS Genlib
[00033.869076]         file format).
[00033.869077] 
[00033.869077]     -constr <file>
[00033.869078]         pass this file with timing constraints to ABC.
[00033.869078]         use with -liberty/-genlib.
[00033.869079] 
[00033.869079]         a constr file contains two lines:
[00033.869080]             set_driving_cell <cell_name>
[00033.869080]             set_load <floating_point_number>
[00033.869081] 
[00033.869081]         the set_driving_cell statement defines which cell type is assumed to
[00033.869082]         drive the primary inputs and the set_load statement sets the load in
[00033.869082]         femtofarads for each primary output.
[00033.869083] 
[00033.869083]     -D <picoseconds>
[00033.869083]         set delay target. the string {D} in the default scripts above is
[00033.869084]         replaced by this option when used, and an empty string otherwise.
[00033.869085]         this also replaces 'dretime' with 'dretime; retime -o {D}' in the
[00033.869091]         default scripts above.
[00033.869092] 
[00033.869092]     -I <num>
[00033.869093]         maximum number of SOP inputs.
[00033.869093]         (replaces {I} in the default scripts above)
[00033.869094] 
[00033.869094]     -P <num>
[00033.869095]         maximum number of SOP products.
[00033.869095]         (replaces {P} in the default scripts above)
[00033.869096] 
[00033.869096]     -lut <width>
[00033.869097]         generate netlist using luts of (max) the specified width.
[00033.869097] 
[00033.869098]     -lut <w1>:<w2>
[00033.869098]         generate netlist using luts of (max) the specified width <w2>. All
[00033.869099]         luts with width <= <w1> have constant cost. for luts larger than <w1>
[00033.869100]         the area cost doubles with each additional input bit. the delay cost
[00033.869100]         is still constant for all lut widths.
[00033.869100] 
[00033.869101]     -luts <cost1>,<cost2>,<cost3>,<sizeN>:<cost4-N>,..
[00033.869102]         generate netlist using luts. Use the specified costs for luts with 1,
[00033.869102]         2, 3, .. inputs.
[00033.869103] 
[00033.869103]     -sop
[00033.869104]         map to sum-of-product cells and inverters
[00033.869104] 
[00033.869105]     -g type1,type2,...
[00033.869105]         Map to the specified list of gate types. Supported gates types are:
[00033.869106]            AND, NAND, OR, NOR, XOR, XNOR, ANDNOT, ORNOT, MUX,
[00033.869106]            NMUX, AOI3, OAI3, AOI4, OAI4.
[00033.869107]         (The NOT gate is always added to this list automatically.)
[00033.869107] 
[00033.869108]         The following aliases can be used to reference common sets of gate
[00033.869108]         types:
[00033.869109]           simple: AND OR XOR MUX
[00033.869109]           cmos2:  NAND NOR
[00033.869110]           cmos3:  NAND NOR AOI3 OAI3
[00033.869110]           cmos4:  NAND NOR AOI3 OAI3 AOI4 OAI4
[00033.869111]           cmos:   NAND NOR AOI3 OAI3 AOI4 OAI4 NMUX MUX XOR XNOR
[00033.869111]           gates:  AND NAND OR NOR XOR XNOR ANDNOT ORNOT
[00033.869112]           aig:    AND NAND OR NOR ANDNOT ORNOT
[00033.869112] 
[00033.869112]         The alias 'all' represent the full set of all gate types.
[00033.869113] 
[00033.869113]         Prefix a gate type with a '-' to remove it from the list. For example
[00033.869114]         the arguments 'AND,OR,XOR' and 'simple,-MUX' are equivalent.
[00033.869114] 
[00033.869115]         The default is 'all,-NMUX,-AOI3,-OAI3,-AOI4,-OAI4'.
[00033.869115] 
[00033.869116]     -dff
[00033.869116]         also pass $_DFF_?_ and $_DFFE_??_ cells through ABC. modules with many
[00033.869117]         clock domains are automatically partitioned in clock domains and each
[00033.869117]         domain is passed through ABC independently.
[00033.869118] 
[00033.869118]     -clk [!]<clock-signal-name>[,[!]<enable-signal-name>]
[00033.869119]         use only the specified clock domain. this is like -dff, but only FF
[00033.869119]         cells that belong to the specified clock domain are used.
[00033.869120] 
[00033.869120]     -keepff
[00033.869121]         set the "keep" attribute on flip-flop output wires. (and thus preserve
[00033.869121]         them, for example for equivalence checking.)
[00033.869121] 
[00033.869122]     -nocleanup
[00033.869123]         when this option is used, the temporary files created by this pass
[00033.869123]         are not removed. this is useful for debugging.
[00033.869123] 
[00033.869124]     -showtmp
[00033.869124]         print the temp dir name in log. usually this is suppressed so that the
[00033.869125]         command output is identical across runs.
[00033.869125] 
[00033.869126]     -markgroups
[00033.869126]         set a 'abcgroup' attribute on all objects created by ABC. The value of
[00033.869127]         this attribute is a unique integer for each ABC process started. This
[00033.869127]         is useful for debugging the partitioning of clock domains.
[00033.869128] 
[00033.869128]     -dress
[00033.869129]         run the 'dress' command after all other ABC commands. This aims to
[00033.869131]         preserve naming by an equivalence check between the original and
[00033.869133]         post-ABC netlists (experimental).
[00033.869133] 
[00033.869134]     -liberty_args <string>
[00033.869134]         when -liberty is used, also pass the specified arguments to ABC
[00033.869135]         command "read_lib". Example: -liberty_args "-G 250"
[00033.869135] 
[00033.869136] When no target cell library is specified the Yosys standard cell library is
[00033.869136] loaded into ABC before the ABC script is executed.
[00033.869137] 
[00033.869137] Note that this is a logic optimization pass within Yosys that is calling ABC
[00033.869138] internally. This is not going to "run ABC on your design". It will instead run
[00033.869138] ABC on logic snippets extracted from your design. You will not get any useful
[00033.869139] output when passing an ABC script that writes a file. Instead write your full
[00033.869139] design as BLIF file with write_blif and then load that into ABC externally if
[00033.869140] you want to use ABC to convert your design into another format.
[00033.869140] 
